  "Question": "How many pairs \\((a,b)\\) of nonzero real numbers satisfy the equation \\[ \\frac1a+\\frac1b=\\frac1{a+b}? \\]",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "none",
    "B": "\\(1\\)",
    "C": "\\(2\\)",
    "D": "one pair for each \\(b\\ne0\\)",
    "E": "two pairs for each \\(b\\ne0\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "A",
  "Solution": "Any solution must satisfy \\(a\\ne0\\), \\(b\\ne0\\), and \\(a+b\\ne0\\), because all three appear as denominators. Multiplying by the nonzero product \\(ab(a+b)\\) gives\n\\[\nb(a+b)+a(a+b)=ab,\n\\]\nso\n\\[\n(a+b)^2=ab.\n\\]\nSince \\(a+b\\ne0\\), its square is positive. Thus this equality forces \\(ab>0\\).\n\nOn the other hand, expanding the same equality and subtracting \\(ab\\) gives\n\\[\na^2+ab+b^2=0.\n\\]\nHere \\(a^2>0\\), \\(b^2>0\\), and we have just shown \\(ab>0\\). Their sum cannot be zero. Therefore no admissible pair exists, and the answer is \\(\\boxed{\\text{A}}\\), none.",
